00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.650
Again, I'm going to start with
kind of some common networking

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.390
concepts, and I'll
map those into what

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.730
their equivalents are inside of
the Google Cloud environment.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:10.330
This is the current state
of Google's global backbone

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.720
network.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.570
So we have our own
private backbone network.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:16.513
It's one of the largest private
backbone networks in the world.

00:02:16.513 --> 00:02:18.430
One of the unique benefits
of cloud networking

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.770
is that within a few minutes,
through configuration

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:24.620
that you define, this basically
becomes your infrastructure.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.670
So you can extend your
on-premise network environment

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.220
literally across the
globe based on which

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.580
cloud regions you deploy compute
and other workloads into.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.092
And that's really the premise
of this presentation, which

00:02:36.092 --> 00:02:38.050
is how do you extend your
on-premise networking

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.850
environment with things
that you're familiar with,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.840
but do so in a way that's
scalable, manageable,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.750
and hopefully with as little
design and operational burden

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.140
as possible.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.400
So again, essentially, this is
basically your backbone network

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.700
completely under your control.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.130
Let's start with some very
familiar kind of constructs.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.280
Though your on-premise data
center environment undoubtedly

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.750
is made up of physical
network devices.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.080
These devices are
commonly purpose specific.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:08.110
So whether we're talking about
a core switch, or an access

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.210
switch, or maybe a
firewall, whether it's

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.630
physical or virtual, these
devices in your network

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.970
are often put together in
a very specific topology

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.950
based on those capabilities.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.900
And where those
devices are placed

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.030
and how you connect them
is very relevant to how

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.240
the network performs and
what capabilities it has.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.700
Now data center topologies
have evolved over time.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.670
You had your traditional three
tier kind of core distribution

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.320
and access networks.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.940
And you had this
resurgence recently.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.460
You have cost-based fabrics
where you have spine and leaf

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:37.450
networks.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.630
But generally speaking in your
on-premise network, topology,

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.030
at least the physical topology,
is generally fairly static.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:45.770
So it doesn't change that much.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.620
So we have the concept
here of the device.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.933
We have the concept of placement
being very relevant, important

00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:52.600
in terms of what those
capabilities are,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.030
and how they apply to the
things that you're actually

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.640
connecting to the network.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.140
Next, we have
different capabilities

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.210
for how you virtualize that
physical infrastructure.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.570
So for example, you have
virtual LANs, or VLANs,

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.700
that allow you to provide
virtual segmentation

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.890
within your physical topology.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.040
You can even have isolation from
an IP forwarding perspective.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.620
So for example, you can
have VRFs, Virtual Routing

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.260
and Forwarding instances,
and historically there's

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.630
been some providers
specific implementations

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:20.570
of these capabilities.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.770
So for example, maybe Virtual
Data Centers, or VDCs,

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.140
and various different types
of overlay and underlay

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.340
technologies.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:28.840
The premise here
is really the same.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.060
How do I basically
virtualize and isolate

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.790
different capabilities on
top of the same physical

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:34.882
infrastructure?

00:04:34.882 --> 00:04:37.090
So here, again, we have the
virtualization concepts--

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.980
VLANs, IP forwarding domains,
like VRFs, and sometimes

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.230
Virtual Data Centers, or VDCs.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.950
Moving on, we have what I refer
to as kind of network identity.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.940
So when you talk about how you
identify systems, particularly

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.830
from a network perspective,
it's no surprise

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.070
we start talking about IP
addressing and subnets,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:59.200
and these kind of exist
pervasively everywhere.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.825
They're in the public
internet, they're

00:05:00.825 --> 00:05:03.748
in your private network, they
obviously exist in the cloud.

00:05:03.748 --> 00:05:06.040
But what I'm specifically
talking about in this context

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.860
is the importance of what
an IP address actually means

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.570
about the identity of a system.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.760
Like, I know these
range of IP addresses

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.220
are my database servers, or
these range of IP addresses

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:17.920
are the DMZ where I
want to kind of apply

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.287
a certain type of
traffic controls.

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:22.120
And placement is also
important here, right?

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.580
Oftentimes where something
connects to the network

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.030
says a lot about what it is and
implies what types of access

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.330
it has or it will allow.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.080
And much like there's
a low rate of change

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.080
with physical networks, there's
typically a pretty low rate

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.768
of change with the number
of subnets and VLANs and IP

00:05:37.768 --> 00:05:39.310
addressing schemes
that you configure

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:40.510
in your on-premise network.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.760
I mean, certainly they
change and expand over time,

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:44.650
but they're generally
not changing

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.440
at a high rate of volume
on a daily or weekly basis.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.370
Now, if we've gotten this far
and your thinking, great, Zach,

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:52.690
IP addresses and subnets.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.790
I'm super happy I
came to your session.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:55.967
Just be patient please.

00:05:55.967 --> 00:05:57.550
I promise we're
actually going to help

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:01.115
you think about these constructs
in a different way in the cloud

00:06:01.115 --> 00:06:03.490
that hopefully makes your
cloud journey a little bit more

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:07.360
simple from a
networking perspective.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.850
Next, we have various different
types of traffic controls

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.150
from a data center perspective.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:12.430
So you have firewalls.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.070
These again can be physical
or virtual devices.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.342
You have network-based
access control lists.

00:06:17.342 --> 00:06:19.550
You may apply these at
various points in the network.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.383
So these are more like
very network-centric.

00:06:21.383 --> 00:06:25.130
You create these based on IP
addresses, subnets, interfaces,

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.285
et cetera.

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:27.910
Sometimes you have
host-based filtering

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:29.702
through agents or other
things you actually

00:06:29.702 --> 00:06:31.330
install on the endpoints.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.730
And finally, more recently,
you have kind of I

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.970
think an emergence of what I
call drop by default fabrics

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.400
or networks, where
multiple hosts are

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.057
connected to the same network.

00:06:41.057 --> 00:06:42.640
And whereas they may
have historically

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.560
been able to automatically
talk to each other

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.630
if they could discover
each other, that's

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:47.620
no longer the case.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:49.120
These networks or
fabrics actually

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.100
drop most unicast
traffic by default,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.010
which requires that
you must explicitly

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:57.580
configure what communication
is allowed across that network.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.370
So I've got here a set of
controls for how I actually

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:03.777
identify and filter traffic.

00:07:03.777 --> 00:07:05.610
So that's where we kind
of came from, right?

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.690
We have the devices.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.120
We have the importance of
placement of those devices

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.010
based on their functionality.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.530
We have the network-based
virtualization technologies.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.730
We talked about VLANs.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.970
We talked about VRFs.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.670
We talked about access
control capabilities.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.580
And so, now it's start to map
these into their equivalents

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:25.297
inside of Google Cloud.

00:07:25.297 --> 00:07:26.880
So the first one is
really the device.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.020
What does a device mean?

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.460
What is the equivalent of
your on-premise network

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:33.210
switch in the Google
Cloud environment?

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.337
And there really isn't one.

00:07:34.337 --> 00:07:36.420
So this is funny, for the
first one there's really

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.430
not a direct equivalent.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.180
And this is really a
byproduct of the fact

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.480
that most everything inside of
the Google Cloud environment

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.380
is heavily software-driven
and globally distributed.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.880
So there's really no call into
I have this network switching

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.010
device, what is its
equivalent in the cloud.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.930
It just doesn't exist.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.640
Now that being
said, we do actually

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.952
surface some
capabilities as devices.

00:07:56.952 --> 00:07:59.160
This is primarily from a
configuration and management

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.097
perspective.

00:08:00.097 --> 00:08:01.680
So for example, when
you're setting up

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.530
routing between your network
and the Google Cloud network,

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.580
you configure that routing using
something called Cloud Router.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:10.620
Now Cloud Router is presented
to you as a thing you configure,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.060
but it's not actually a device.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.310
Behind the scenes it's
actually a distributed set

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.130
of software processes that
live within a particular cloud

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:17.707
region.

00:08:17.707 --> 00:08:19.540
So you're not actually
configuring a device.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.290
You're basically
providing a configuration

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.450
that we then program these
software processes with

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.720
and distribute them
across the infrastructure.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.790
In a similar fashion, you
have the VPC firewall,

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.610
the native firewall built
into the virtual private cloud

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:33.990
in Google Cloud.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.450
We present this to you from
a configuration perspective

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:38.880
as a single global
list of rules.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:40.419
So it's very simple
to configure,

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:43.272
you can kind of see everything
in one list or one page.

00:08:43.272 --> 00:08:44.730
But in reality,
what we're doing is

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:46.590
we're taking those rules
that you configure,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.965
and we're actually pushing
them down and programming them

00:08:48.965 --> 00:08:51.750
at the host level on
the individual machines

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.340
where your virtual
machines are scheduled.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:56.593
So really no equivalent
from a device perspective,

00:08:56.593 --> 00:08:58.260
but we do kind of
manifest that in a way

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.540
that's convenient for you
for both configuration

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.240
and management.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.330
Next is mapping for network.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.280
So we have our
physical network, we

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.657
have virtual networks in
our on-premise environment.

00:09:09.657 --> 00:09:11.240
What's the equivalent
in Google Cloud?

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.920
And this is the VPC,
Virtual Private Cloud.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.550
This is our global
VPC, and it's really

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:19.520
kind of a direct correlation
with your IP forwarding domain

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:20.090
on-prem.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.910
A lot of customers have
just a single IP forwarding

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.990
domain, which is you have one
routing space, one IP space,

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:29.480
and there's a lot of
similarities between that

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:30.930
and the VPC in the cloud.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:34.190
So for example, the IP
addressing inside of a VPC

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:36.380
and inside one of your
forwarding domains on-prem

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.580
has to be unique.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.520
You generally avoid
duplicate IP addressing.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:43.250
Likewise, you don't generally
automatically connect

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:46.382
to networks together without
some explicit configuration.

00:09:46.382 --> 00:09:47.840
So in your on-prem
environment, you

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.500
may establish connections
between networks.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.120
You may establish BGP routing
protocols between networks

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.880
to actually explicitly
make communication happen.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:57.830
Very similar in the
VPC environment--

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.440
VPCs are isolated
in that they do not

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:02.960
communicate with other
VPCs inside the cloud

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.300
unless you do something
explicitly to make that happen.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.070
One interesting
thing about the VPC

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:12.590
is it's a global
construct in Google Cloud.

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:16.010
And what that means is
that the scope of the VPC,

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.740
how big of a geographic
area it actually covers,

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.720
is completely dependent
on how you deploy

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.420
workloads in the cloud.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.920
So for example, if you start
out by deploying your workloads

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.767
in one cloud region, that's
really the scope of your VPC.

00:10:27.767 --> 00:10:29.600
But as soon as you start
deploying workloads

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.120
in other cloud regions
across the globe,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.580
the network's scope
immediately expands

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.260
to include that entire network.

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.510
And so this is actually
pretty unique to Google Cloud,

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.790
and it's actually a really,
really nice implementation

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:42.890
because it allows you to
basically instantly grow

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.833
your network in global
scope, kind of going back

00:10:45.833 --> 00:10:47.750
to that global backbone
slide that I presented

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.350
at the beginning, with a single
routing and firewall policy.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.230
So your network, your
VRFs on-prem, they

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:58.440
map into VPCs in the
cloud environment.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.380
Next, we have the
concept of VLANs.

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:03.018
So we only use VLANs in a very
limited fashion in the cloud.

00:11:03.018 --> 00:11:05.060
We don't use them actually
within the data center

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.602
switching fabric, meaning you
don't put your virtual machines

00:11:07.602 --> 00:11:09.530
inside of a specific VLAN.

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:13.040
We only use VLANs to actually
virtualize the connectivity

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.732
back to your
on-premise environment.

00:11:14.732 --> 00:11:16.190
This is specifically
with a product

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:17.840
we have called Cloud
Interconnect that

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:20.330
allows you to have
high speed, low latency

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.010
access from your network
to Google's network.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.220
But you can actually create
multiple virtual links

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.650
across those physical
circuits and terminate

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:30.380
those in different VPCs
in different locations

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:31.560
across the world.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:32.060
That's it.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.230
There's no VLANs anywhere
else in our environment.

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.180
There's no VLAN numbering.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.638
You need to think
about making sure

00:11:36.638 --> 00:11:41.630
that you put access lists or map
interfaces to a certain VLAN.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:45.480
That just doesn't exist in
the cloud environment today.

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:46.890
Next, we have subnets.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:48.870
So show of hands,
who in the room

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.640
remembers deploying
workloads in Google

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.780
Cloud before we had
VPCs and subnets?

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:54.545
Anyone?

00:11:54.545 --> 00:11:56.578
A few people.

00:11:56.578 --> 00:11:57.120
So it's true.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.160
It used to be one
big flat IP space

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.890
across that entire global
backbone I mentioned.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:03.420
But as customers
started connecting

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.348
to our environment in more
different geographic locations,

00:12:06.348 --> 00:12:07.890
we needed the ability
to provide them

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.800
with optimized access
from a routing perspective

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.480
to the closest
cloud region where

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.160
they're deploying workloads.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:17.040
And that's really the
purpose of the subnet inside

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.840
of Google Cloud.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.610
I would advise you
to think about it

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:22.950
as less of an
isolation mechanism,

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:24.690
less of a segmentation
mechanism,

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:28.620
and more of an identity
regionally for instances

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.060
that you deploy in
certain cloud regions.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.030
So for example, you
can deploy resources

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.190
in a cloud region
on a single subnet

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:36.690
so long as that
subnet is big enough

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.700
from an IP addressing
perspective to handle

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:39.880
all of your workloads.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:41.340
So all of your
Kubernetes clusters,

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.205
all of your virtual
machines, et cetera.

00:12:43.205 --> 00:12:44.580
That's really the
genesis, that's

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.110
the primary purpose of subnets.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.170
That are really not intended to
be kind of a first-class entity

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.730
to identify systems.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.220
It's about efficient routing
between your environment

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.630
and our environment.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.830
So your subnets, they map
into subnets in Google Cloud,

00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:59.580
but the purpose of
Google Cloud subnets

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.400
is really about regional
identity or regional proximity

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.730
of resources.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.090
Then we have IP addresses.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.253
So our IP addresses
within your VPC,

00:13:08.253 --> 00:13:09.420
they're original constructs.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.180
So you create a subnet
within a particular region,

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.808
and it has a unique set of
IP addresses for that region.

00:13:14.808 --> 00:13:16.350
We also have public
IP addresses that

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.142
are used for our global
load balancers that

00:13:18.142 --> 00:13:19.440
are internet-facing.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.420
For the regional
IP addresses, these

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.820
are by default automatically
managed by the cloud.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.370
So we automatically allocate
IP addresses to machines,

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.380
we automatically
allocate IP addresses

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.810
to Kubernetes pods, et cetera.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.150
So I want to actually
ask you to do something.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:34.810
As you kind of go
through this session,

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.560
I want you to think about what
if you don't have the ability

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.230
to explicitly define the
IP address for a given

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:41.780
virtual machine?

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:43.530
This is already very
common in Kubernetes.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.600
You can't specify that
a Kubernetes pod has

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.520
an explicit static IP address.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.830
Assume you can't do that
with a virtual machine.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.550
How are you going to
handle forwarding?

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.610
How are you going to
handle access control,

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.230
micro segmentation?

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.688
We're going to talk about some
of these things a little bit

00:13:57.688 --> 00:13:58.360
later.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:00.870
And finally, the equivalent
for the firewalls

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.190
in your environment or in
general access control,

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:04.630
we have a number of products.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.840
These are, again,
globally distributed,

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.790
and they really kind of
span layer three controls,

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.900
so building access controls
based on IP addressing,

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.700
all the way up to
layer seven controls.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:17.730
So does this user, who's
part of this group,

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.770
have the right access
to actually interface

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:21.360
with this cloud API?

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.892
So there's a complete
spectrum of access controls

00:14:23.892 --> 00:14:25.350
that cover a lot
of different parts

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.183
of the stack from the
network all the way up

00:14:27.183 --> 00:14:29.742
to the application level.

00:14:29.742 --> 00:14:31.200
So again, this is
important when we

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:34.440
start talking about
segmentation and the ability

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.840
to kind of abstract how
we identify and control

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:38.670
a system, separate from
where it's actually

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.328
placed in the network.

00:14:40.328 --> 00:14:42.120
And so with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:44.495
to Matt, who's going to talk
about some common enterprise

00:14:44.495 --> 00:14:47.230
design scenarios and how
we can approach those.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.380
MATT NOWINA: Thank
you very much, Zach.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.660
I don't know how many of
you are public presenters,

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.000
but this is normally
the part where

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:56.168
you get stuck looking
into the lights

00:14:56.168 --> 00:14:57.960
and forget everything
that you've prepared.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:01.680
But what we really want to
talk about at this next stage

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:06.060
is how do we start mapping
these analogs into Google Cloud

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.100
solutions specifically.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.980
So here you get a
snapshot of what

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:12.780
the Google Cloud
networking product

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.750
portfolio looks like today.

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:17.430
There's 20+ products
and services all focused

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:21.900
on enabling your
journey to the cloud.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.315
We've grouped these into
different sections that

00:15:24.315 --> 00:15:26.190
represent the way that
customers are thinking

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:30.000
about the cloud-- so connecting,
scale, optimize, security,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.720
and modernize.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.930
And so, what we're going
to do in this next section

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:36.900
is we're going to touch through
a series of these products

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:40.570
and sort of answer questions
that customers come to us with.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.570
So in this section, what
we're going to focus on

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:46.740
are the Cloud
Interconnect VPN and VPCs.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.080
So one of the first
things that customers

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.670
think about when
they're coming to us

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:54.870
is how do I take advantage
of this new network.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.310
Oftentimes when you have
on-premise environments,

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.930
you have fixed infrastructure
and fixed locations,

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:04.170
but you don't have the way a
way to extend your applications

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:08.170
to where your customers are,
improve the speed and latency.

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:10.470
And so, that's where, as
Zach was mentioning before,

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.790
global CPC comes into play.

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:15.390
This is a way of
simplifying the ability

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.660
to connect to all of the
regions where you deploy things,

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.210
makes it simple for you
to enable replication

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:24.030
across your applications,
as well as leveraging

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:26.490
Google-managed services that
are built from the ground

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:31.800
up on a multi-region deployment
model and the associated

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.860
availability.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.290
The next thing, once we've
started to map out the network,

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:40.250
we start to think about,
well, how do we fit this

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:42.470
into our operational model.

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:44.570
So by show of hands,
how many of you

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:49.300
no longer have dedicated
networking or security teams?

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:50.530
So there's a few.

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:52.170
And we know the
industry is moving

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:55.560
towards DevOps models
or DevSecOps models,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:58.580
but until we fully
move into that,

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.960
we have to think
about how can we

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.300
take our workflows in our
on-premise environment today

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.170
and map those over to the cloud.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.810
This is where shared
VPC comes into play.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:10.619
So shared VPC is
designed around the idea

00:17:10.619 --> 00:17:13.290
that we have different teams
that deploy our applications

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.079
and different teams that
manage our networks.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:19.200
And we don't just want to give
free rein to the application

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:21.180
teams to deploy networks
as they see fit.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.680
We still want to have
some level of control.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:26.220
So shared VPC is
enterprise friendly.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.680
It's a centralized
model, and it allows

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.270
you to centralize
your administration

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:31.650
and auditability.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:33.580
So now we've got a global VPC.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.280
We have a deployment
model that works.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.820
As Zach mentioned before,
we want to look at--

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:40.530
I mean, we're a
hybrid enterprise.

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.900
How are we going to
interconnect into GCP?

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:47.280
And it's important to note
that you need different models

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.860
depending upon what
you're going to rely

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:52.080
on that hybrid connectivity for.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.030
Are you just doing management?

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:57.725
Do you need to do batch data
loads at certain times of day?

00:17:57.725 --> 00:17:59.850
Or, is this going to become
a mission critical part

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:01.120
of the application?

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:05.010
This is where things like
Cloud VPN, Cloud Interconnect

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:09.390
come into play, and being
able to map your requirements

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:14.110
and your costs to the
exact implementation.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.960
So what does it look
like when we start

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.100
to put these things together?

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:19.950
This is an example--

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.410
a zoomed in, simplified
example here-- but starting

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:25.440
on the left-hand side, you
can see an on-premises network

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:30.720
with four dedicated connections
coming into Google Cloud.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.510
These are coming into
two separate regions

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:37.560
that are both accessible
through the global VPC.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.600
That VPC is stored within
a shared VPC host project,

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.090
and we can share
individual subnets out

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.260
to the service projects.

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:49.350
So again, it's a
relatively simple way

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:51.360
of leveraging
physical connectivity,

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:54.240
ensuring a four nines
SLA, and giving you

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:57.070
centralized administration.

00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:59.890
So from here, one of the
other common questions

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:03.330
that we get, and I was sort
of confused when I first

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.080
heard this, but there's
some customers who

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:09.250
are surprised to hear that our
managed services are typically

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:12.750
hosted on the internet,
on external IPs.

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:15.840
Now Zach and I would probably
be among the first to argue

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:19.500
that simply putting
something on a public IP

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:23.710
really is about accessibility
and not security.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.610
But for customers
that have invested

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.830
in these interconnects,
that want

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:31.090
to leverage this private
connectivity for accessing

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:32.790
the managed services,
it makes sense

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:34.710
to have an option to do so.

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:38.250
And so, that's where we start to
introduce private Google access

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.690
and private service access.

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:43.590
So what these are are ways
of extending those managed

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:46.920
services to your
on-prem environment.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.050
I'll pause as people
take pictures.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:52.020
But what it really
looks like is this.

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:55.170
So that same interconnect
model that you had before,

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:59.100
what you're now
able to do is to--

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.850
as a part of your dynamic
peering with Google,

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:05.250
you're able to advertise
a restricted VIP back

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.210
to your on-premises environment.

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:10.650
So this is a special IP range
that will come from your VPC

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.510
to your on-premises environment.

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:14.370
And then through
the use of DNS you

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:18.640
can swing services over
to that restricted VIP.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.850
The three basic models
for four using this are--

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.690
the first is to do an
enterprise-wide scening

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:29.350
of start at GoogleAPIs.com
to the restricted VIP.

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:32.980
If you don't want this to
apply to all of your services,

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:36.250
what you may choose to do
is implemented DNS view.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:39.760
So a view only for
certain clients

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.010
implementing the exact
same redirection,

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:46.102
or you can even implement
it as a host based level.

00:20:46.102 --> 00:20:47.560
So we've talked
about connectivity.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.830
Now I want to spend just
a few minutes on security,

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.880
and more specifically
on our Cloud Armor IP,

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:58.470
VPC firewall rules, and
cloud firewall rules.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:01.790
So no one is going to consider
leveraging a cloud service

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:03.860
provider if they don't
have complete faith

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:05.930
in the implementation
of firewalling.

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:10.280
GCP VPC firewalls provide
a micro segmentation model,

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.740
because they are
effectively implemented

00:21:12.740 --> 00:21:14.210
at the host-based level.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:17.420
What that means is two instances
running on the same physical

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:20.660
host cannot connect to one
another without traversing that

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:21.590
firewall.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:26.180
And the default stance of that
firewall is a deny ingress.

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.820
At the same time, these
rules are stateful.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.300
So they're simpler to maintain
then more traditional stateless

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:33.320
ACLs.

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:35.510
And we can see the
segmentation model implemented

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:37.700
in the following slide.

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:40.540
So again, this is a
relatively simplified model

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:43.090
here, but when I started
thinking about this--

00:21:43.090 --> 00:21:44.240
I have a question.

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.920
How many people have
had to implement 802.1x

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.270
in an on-premise environment.

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:51.970
So there's a few people.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.950
This was port-based
network access control.

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:58.660
So this was introduced at a
time when we no longer knew

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:01.255
what endpoint was going
to plug into what switch,

00:22:01.255 --> 00:22:03.130
so where in the network
they might be, and be

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.890
able to dynamically configure
that port with the associated

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:07.790
rules for that endpoint.

00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:11.620
Well, the same thing can be
done with CPC firewall rules.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.320
Through the use of tags
or service accounts,

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:18.220
you can have your
endpoints inherit the rules

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:22.850
that they need in order to
access the correct endpoints.

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:26.010
And you can do this
very similar to 802.1x,

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.740
but without the pain of
having to manage TACACS.

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.770
So with this combination
we have granular rules

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:36.610
that are applied
dynamically within our VPC,

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:38.920
and then as we start to
push out towards the edge,

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:42.430
we have Cloud Armor and
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:46.000
Cloud Armor will extend our
defense in depth strategy

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:51.550
by adding layer seven DDOS and
web application firewalling

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:52.900
to our apps.

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:57.760
And IAP provides a way of
extending our applications

00:22:57.760 --> 00:22:59.810
to only specific users.

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:02.170
In fact, we also
introduced quite recently

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:03.880
a blog post that
talks about using

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:06.340
IAP in place of bastion hosts.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:10.090
So now you can open up 22
to your individual hosts

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:12.280
that you want to manage,
but ensure that's only

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:15.730
exposed through to
certain identities

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:19.720
without the need of having
a separate management host.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:23.170
Then the next part, and this
is a really critical one

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:26.410
because we've all heard
repeatedly these stories

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:29.980
about misconfigurations
of access rules

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:33.880
on a managed service
that end up exposing data

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:35.170
to unauthorized clients.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:39.310
So how do we address this?

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:43.280
Well, Google's actually taken
a two-pronged approach to this.

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:46.090
The first is a set of open
source security tools called

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:50.080
Forseti, which give you the
ability to establish inventory

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:54.190
policies and remediation
actions whenever changes are

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:56.660
made within your environment.

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:59.740
And the second is
VPC Service Controls,

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:02.680
which allows you to establish
a trusted perimeter model

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:05.600
around your VPCs and projects.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:08.660
So this is what it
looks like in practice.

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:14.320
So here we have a project in
the VPC, and the associated

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:16.600
services that we
want to protect.

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:18.520
We also, through
the same mechanism

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:21.610
of that private Google
access, have the ability

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:24.820
to extend these services to
our on-premises environment

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.770
and say, that's a part
of our trusted perimeter.

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:30.280
So when VPC Service
Controls is enabled,

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:32.620
only resources from
within the perimeter

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:34.630
can interact with
those services,

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:37.420
and they can't be
used to copy those

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:40.540
to any external
unauthorized projects

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.460
and prevent access
from the internet.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:47.220
So this protects against
that misconfiguration.

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:50.430
Lastly, all of these
security controls are great,

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:54.540
but centralized logging and SIM
solutions are not going away.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.180
So this is where VPC
flow logs and firewall

00:24:57.180 --> 00:24:58.860
logs come into play.

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:02.160
The VPC flow logs provide
you with NetFlow style data,

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.530
TCP dumped information
without the payload

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:07.560
that gives you information
about the flows within your VPC

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:08.340
environment.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:09.960
And firewall logs
gives you insight

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:16.068
into what's being allowed and
blocked by your VPC firewalls.

00:25:16.068 --> 00:25:20.070
So now that you have a sense
of the various products,

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:22.590
the analogs to the on-prem,
what the options are

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:26.250
in GCP, what we
want to do is try

00:25:26.250 --> 00:25:28.260
something a little different.

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.030
As network
specialists, Zach and I

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.190
get the chance to see
many different customer

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:33.163
configurations.

00:25:33.163 --> 00:25:35.580
And then this next section,
we wanted to try and verbalize

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:37.372
the thought process
that we go through when

00:25:37.372 --> 00:25:41.160
considering different sets
of customer requirements.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.990
Before we start
that, I just wanted

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:46.380
to give you a few quick
points in terms of VPC design

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.730
pre-work and recommendations.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:53.820
So the first is identifying
who your stakeholders are.

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:55.800
This can vary depending
upon who you're

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.090
trying to design this VPC for.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:00.620
Is it for an individual
application, a line

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:02.980
of business, your
entire organization?

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:04.720
It's important to
understand who you're

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:06.720
trying to address, and
make sure that you really

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:08.950
understand their requirements.

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:13.500
The second is to start with
security objectives and not

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:14.850
security controls.

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:18.690
Many times we see
customers come to GCP

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:23.160
and say, how do I do X in GCP,
rather than thinking about why

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:26.610
they're doing X. So by starting
with the security objectives,

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.140
you have a very
clear understanding

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:29.848
of what you're trying
to achieve and what

00:26:29.848 --> 00:26:32.410
your options are in the cloud.

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:36.550
The next is understanding how
many VPCs you're going to need.

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.270
And I don't mean coming
up with a static number,

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.970
like 5, 6, 7, 10.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:44.520
The important thing
here is to understand

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:48.180
what you're trying to achieve,
where your scale and quota

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:50.640
limits are going
to play, and get

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:54.380
an overall understanding
of what magnitude you're

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:56.710
going to have to address.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:59.170
And lastly, think simple.

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:01.870
Don't design things
just because you can.

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:06.100
I mean, we all know that
simplicity is directly

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:07.600
correlated with supportability.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:10.600
So keeping it to
exactly what you need

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:11.830
is going to be important.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:13.523
Anything you want to
add on this slide?

00:27:13.523 --> 00:27:15.940
ZACH SEILS: I think the point
of number three is relevant.

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:17.480
It's not a static number
you're trying to get.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:18.855
It's really a
pattern that you're

00:27:18.855 --> 00:27:21.563
trying to use so that you can
grow and scale your network

00:27:21.563 --> 00:27:23.230
environment inside
the cloud in the most

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.970
efficient manner possible.

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:30.550
MATT NOWINA: So let's start
with a simple scenario here.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:33.622
Zach, what do you see in
this initial scenario?

00:27:33.622 --> 00:27:34.330
ZACH SEILS: Sure.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:38.068
So again, the idea here
is this is basically

00:27:38.068 --> 00:27:38.860
our day job, right?

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:42.140
So how do we kind of think
about approaching this out loud?

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:44.980
So obviously here I've
got a single VPC, global,

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:46.300
pretty straightforward.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:48.550
Looks like I have both
development and production

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:50.455
workloads in the same VPC.

00:27:50.455 --> 00:27:51.830
They're across
different regions,

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:54.250
so they're in different subnets.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:56.860
Single project, which
is also relevant when

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:59.602
we look at how we scale out.

00:27:59.602 --> 00:28:01.060
And the other thing
that's apparent

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.790
here is it looks to me
like this is predominantly

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:05.390
a cloud isolated workload.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:07.900
I don't see any hybrid
connectivity back

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:09.198
to the on-premises environment.

00:28:09.198 --> 00:28:11.740
So those are kind of the things
that initially pop out at me.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:13.420
MATT NOWINA: Any
best practices you

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:15.502
think we can take
away from this design?

00:28:15.502 --> 00:28:16.210
ZACH SEILS: Yeah.

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:18.730
I mean, I think a couple here
that really resonate with me,

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:21.402
something you just mentioned,
which is start out simple.

00:28:21.402 --> 00:28:23.110
So I think this is a
very common approach

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:25.150
that customers who are
either new to cloud

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.700
can take just so they kind of
get their bearings in the cloud

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:28.665
environment.

00:28:28.665 --> 00:28:30.790
Or, even customers that
are coming from other cloud

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:32.207
providers, and
they're just trying

00:28:32.207 --> 00:28:33.760
to get familiar
and have experience

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:37.580
with how some of our networking
constructs may differ.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:40.210
So for example, the global
VPC, how does it actually

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:41.870
behave in practice?

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:44.150
So simplicity is a key one here.

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:47.770
I think the other one
here, too, is just

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:51.080
the small number of subnets
with larger address ranges.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.620
There's no really reason to
kind of over-rotate and start

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:55.840
creating a bunch of
subnets within a region.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:58.093
You can start with one with
adequate IP address space.

00:28:58.093 --> 00:29:00.010
We have a great feature
where you can actually

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:02.483
grow the size of
your existing subnets

00:29:02.483 --> 00:29:04.900
in a completely hitless manner
to the VMs that are already

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:05.860
deployed.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:08.500
This doesn't preclude you from
creating multiple subnets.

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:11.000
This is just what we see and
what we recommend for customers

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:12.500
who are just getting started.

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:14.380
One nice thing
here is that things

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:17.770
are relatively programmatic
and easy to deploy and manage

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:19.690
inside of Google Cloud.

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:21.530
So even for core
infrastructure, stuff

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:23.530
that takes a lot of
planning and a lot of effort

00:29:23.530 --> 00:29:25.120
to implement in an
on-premises environment,

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.370
they can actually be pretty
disposable and pretty easy

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:29.218
to kind of delete
and recreate inside

00:29:29.218 --> 00:29:30.260
of the cloud environment.

00:29:30.260 --> 00:29:33.212
So I think overall a
good starting point.

00:29:33.212 --> 00:29:34.670
One thing I do
notice here, though,

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:37.870
and I think a pretty common
recommendation as customers

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:40.090
start to scale in
the cloud, is really

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:43.240
kind of starting to segregate or
put more kind of firm isolation

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:45.545
between development and
production workloads.

00:29:45.545 --> 00:29:47.170
MATT NOWINA: So you
mean something that

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:49.790
looks a little more like this?

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:52.450
So in this design,
there is a couple

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:54.370
of things that jump
out to me right away.

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:57.610
I mean, the first is
exactly what you've

00:29:57.610 --> 00:29:59.080
sort of identified there.

00:29:59.080 --> 00:30:02.740
We've moved from a subnet
model as an isolation

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.200
boundary between our
different environments,

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.400
and into VPC level.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:10.780
So at VPC level, we've now
segregated firewall rule

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:13.730
management into
two separate VPCs.

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:15.550
And then the other
big one that jumps out

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:17.650
is the hybrid connectivity.

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:19.480
So here we can
see that we've now

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:22.900
deployed cloud routers,
dedicated interconnect,

00:30:22.900 --> 00:30:25.720
and VLAN attachments into
each of the individual VPCs.

00:30:29.112 --> 00:30:29.820
ZACH SEILS: Yeah.

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.340
I mean, just a point kind of
on the hybrid connectivity.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.800
If you notice here, we have
actually separate connectivity

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:38.140
coming from on-premises
into each VPC.

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:39.900
And this has virtual
connectivity.

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:42.688
So if you're using
VPN, for example,

00:30:42.688 --> 00:30:43.980
these are separate VPN tunnels.

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:45.397
If you're using
interconnect, this

00:30:45.397 --> 00:30:48.900
can be the same physical circuit
that you pair Google's network

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:51.630
on, but different logical
connections, those interconnect

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:53.380
attachments I
mentioned previously

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:55.080
that terminate in the
separate projects.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:58.082
You kind of extend the isolation
of the different environments

00:30:58.082 --> 00:30:59.790
all the way through
the connectivity back

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:01.170
to your on-premises environment.

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:04.267
So it's really a clean kind of
separation which works well.

00:31:04.267 --> 00:31:06.600
MATT NOWINA: And as you've
mentioned in previous slides,

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:09.960
because we now have
separate independent VPCs

00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:12.600
and there is no inherent
connectivity between them,

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:15.555
we need to start thinking
about how does our application

00:31:15.555 --> 00:31:17.430
deployment look, where
are our build servers,

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.700
where are we actually
deploying from.

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:21.880
If we're deploying from
on-premises, this works.

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.710
We've got connectivity
between them.

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:26.610
But if the build servers are
sitting in one of those VPCs,

00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:28.440
we now have to
start to look at VPC

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:30.540
peering or connecting
those VPCs together

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:34.080
in order to allow for that build
process or that deploy process

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:35.410
to succeed.

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:37.800
And when we start to think
about those peering, that's

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:39.610
what we need to
start thinking about,

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:41.940
well, what are the aggregate
resource requirements

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.160
when we mesh these
two VPCs together.

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:49.380
So when you think about
this design, what's

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:51.780
the next logical extension?

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:53.740
Where do people go from here?

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:56.370
Especially if we
wanted to sort of align

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:58.912
with that workflow
framework from earlier.

00:31:58.912 --> 00:31:59.620
ZACH SEILS: Yeah.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:01.680
So one thing I noticed
when Matt asked

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:03.940
who doesn't have network
or security teams anymore,

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:05.383
there was like a few hands.

00:32:05.383 --> 00:32:07.800
And so, if I assume the inverse,
I assume that most of you

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:08.340
still do.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:11.580
And so, one thing that we see
if we go to the next slide

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:14.800
is this concept of shared
VPC that Matt talked about.

00:32:14.800 --> 00:32:16.500
And so, to me,
shared VPC is as much

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:19.290
an organizational construct
as it is a set of technologies

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:20.830
that you leverage in the cloud.

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:22.620
And what I mean by
that is that shared VPC

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.120
is designed for
organizations where

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:27.270
you want to maintain
centralized administration

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:30.240
and control of the network
and the security functions

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:31.310
in the cloud.

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:32.760
And so, shared VPC does that.

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:36.790
Shared VPC is still
fundamentally a single VPC,

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:38.370
but it's a single
VPC that can be

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:40.920
leveraged by multiple
projects, and it

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:43.810
has a curated set of
permissions, IEM permissions.

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:46.560
So there is a specific role for
network admins in the shared

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.810
VPC, and they control,
as you might imagine,

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:52.110
creating subnets, establishing
hybrid connectivity,

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:53.880
establishing routing policies.

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:56.790
There is an explicit
role for security admins

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:58.770
in the shared VPC
model, who control

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:00.790
firewall rules, et cetera.

00:33:00.790 --> 00:33:03.007
So if your organization
is structured in that way,

00:33:03.007 --> 00:33:04.590
and that's an
organizational construct

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:06.540
you intend to carry
over into the cloud,

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:10.057
the shared VPC is a really
nice model for this.

00:33:10.057 --> 00:33:12.640
So here you can see we've got a
couple of things in play here.

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:15.690
We've got actually
multiple different VPCs all

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.620
still within a single project.

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:20.950
And then the shared VPC model,
we call this a host project.

00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:23.760
So your host project has all
of your networking and security

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:24.600
stuff--

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:27.510
the VPC, the firewall
rules, the connectivity back

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:29.410
to your on-premises
environments.

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:31.740
And then you have one or
more service projects.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:34.170
Service projects are
separate projects

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.840
that are usually given to the
application or development

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:37.770
teams.

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:40.560
In those separate projects, they
can spin up their own compute,

00:33:40.560 --> 00:33:42.690
they can spin up their
own Kubernetes clusters,

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:45.960
they have the autonomy to manage
their workloads themselves.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.350
And these service projects
attach to the shared VPC

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.310
to leverage those resources.

00:33:51.310 --> 00:33:53.670
So the networking and security
teams maintain control

00:33:53.670 --> 00:33:56.068
over the network and security,
the application teams,

00:33:56.068 --> 00:33:58.110
they manage their workloads,
they manage compute,

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:02.510
they manage Kubernetes
clusters, et cetera.

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:04.650
So I mean, in terms
of best practices,

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:07.260
just thinking kind of thinking
about this a little bit--

00:34:07.260 --> 00:34:09.389
number one is I like simplicity.

00:34:09.389 --> 00:34:10.920
So we've got a
pretty simple model.

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:14.955
It's still the single VPC
kind of model, if you will,

00:34:14.955 --> 00:34:16.830
which again kind of just
reinforces the point

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:18.288
that we want to
kind start to think

00:34:18.288 --> 00:34:23.190
about non-networking constructs
as the primary identifier

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:23.844
for workload.

00:34:23.844 --> 00:34:25.469
So we don't want just
the fact that you

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:27.929
happen to be connected to
a VPC to say everything

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:29.330
about what you can do.

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:31.622
There's a better way to
address that, which we're going

00:34:31.622 --> 00:34:33.342
to talk about in a little bit.

00:34:33.342 --> 00:34:34.800
Another nice thing
about shared VPC

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:36.690
is you actually have
pretty granular control

00:34:36.690 --> 00:34:40.290
on which portions of the VPC
are visible to the service

00:34:40.290 --> 00:34:41.110
projects.

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:43.800
So for example, you can say
this particular group of service

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:47.969
projects for service
XYZ, they can only

00:34:47.969 --> 00:34:52.072
see this particular subnet
or set of subnets in the VPC.

00:34:52.072 --> 00:34:53.489
So you can kind
of help people not

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:55.650
make mistakes in terms of
deploying their workloads

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:57.637
in the wrong location.

00:34:57.637 --> 00:34:58.720
So this works really well.

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:00.000
It's actually a
very common thing.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.830
It's pretty popular with
enterprise customers,

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:03.247
mostly, as I
mentioned, because of

00:35:03.247 --> 00:35:04.883
that organizational alignment.

00:35:04.883 --> 00:35:07.050
But a question, I guess,
back for you is what if you

00:35:07.050 --> 00:35:08.400
need to kind of scale this out?

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:11.340
Because fundamentally we're
dealing with a single VPC,

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:13.050
what if things go
really well and you

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:15.660
need to kind of scale up
to maybe tens of thousands

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:17.702
of virtual machine instances?

00:35:17.702 --> 00:35:19.410
MATT NOWINA: Yeah, so
I think that's when

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:23.790
we start to see a move towards
this model, where now we

00:35:23.790 --> 00:35:27.150
are separating out
the host projects

00:35:27.150 --> 00:35:30.170
and going with a single
VPC per host project.

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:34.030
This allows us to
more accurately align

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:38.380
VPC and project quotas to
an individual host project,

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:41.183
and allow them to scale
independently of one another.

00:35:41.183 --> 00:35:42.850
So no longer are you
going to be worried

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:47.140
about if a dev resource
spins up things

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:50.200
that your project relies
upon, because now they're

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.090
managed independently.

00:35:52.090 --> 00:35:56.080
The other big thing that this
design starts to introduce

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:58.880
is segregation at the IEM level.

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:02.170
So in the previous models when
you're using the security admin

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:05.380
role, within that
host project you

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:08.140
would have had the ability
to modify firewall rules

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:10.030
across any of the VPCs.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:13.160
In this model they're now
independent of one another.

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:15.700
So we can have different users
mapped to each of those host

00:36:15.700 --> 00:36:20.090
projects, and we're starting to
see a scale out pattern here.

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:23.680
What we're talking about is
building that host project

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:25.390
segregation at the
environment level.

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:27.330
So prod, test, dev.

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:30.530
But we could continue to
make this more granular

00:36:30.530 --> 00:36:32.500
if our application
requirements demand it,

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:35.170
where we can create host
projects on individual line

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:36.315
of business or application.

00:36:36.315 --> 00:36:37.690
ZACH SEILS: So
just so I'm clear,

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:40.065
I just want to make sure I
understood one thing you said.

00:36:40.065 --> 00:36:41.890
So the segregation of
the IEM permissions,

00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:43.300
that's because the
permissions are associated

00:36:43.300 --> 00:36:44.230
at the project level?

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:44.660
MATT NOWINA: Correct.

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:45.100
ZACH SEILS: OK.

00:36:45.100 --> 00:36:46.818
So by moving into
separate projects here,

00:36:46.818 --> 00:36:49.360
you're giving your ability to
kind of delegate administration

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:50.770
to different parts of
the cloud environment.

00:36:50.770 --> 00:36:52.010
MATT NOWINA: That's right.

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:55.060
As we continue to move out
in this scale model, what

00:36:55.060 --> 00:36:57.460
you can see is now
we are increasing

00:36:57.460 --> 00:36:59.620
the number of cloud
routers, increasing

00:36:59.620 --> 00:37:01.390
the number of VLAN attachments.

00:37:01.390 --> 00:37:03.970
And while it's a software
defined network on the Google

00:37:03.970 --> 00:37:05.770
Cloud side, on the
on-premises side

00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:07.603
we need to think about
how we're going to be

00:37:07.603 --> 00:37:09.410
managing those connections.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:12.340
So what if it's a pain
on the on-premises side?

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:14.303
What can we do to help
optimize for that?

00:37:14.303 --> 00:37:15.970
ZACH SEILS: Yeah,
it's a great question.

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.083
It's a frequent feedback
we hear from customers.

00:37:18.083 --> 00:37:19.000
It's like, it's great.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.960
The network in the cloud
is software defined.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:24.220
I can spin things up and delete
things with relative ease.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:27.080
But when it starts to talk
about hybrid interconnectivity

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:28.600
with your on-premises
environment,

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:30.562
that may not always
be the case right so

00:37:30.562 --> 00:37:32.020
getting the
appropriate permissions

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:35.410
to change configuration add
additional interfaces create

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.310
new BGP peers.

00:37:37.310 --> 00:37:38.935
Sometimes customers
want to avoid this.

00:37:38.935 --> 00:37:40.560
And what they're
really after basically

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:43.360
is trying to leverage not only
the same physical connectivity

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:46.450
with the Google Cloud network,
but the same logical or virtual

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:48.540
connections with the
Google Cloud network.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:50.290
And so, we move on to
a different scenario

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:53.260
here, which the actual
VPC structure here, which

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:56.710
dev test and prod in
multiple projects, that

00:37:56.710 --> 00:37:58.225
really stays the same.

00:37:58.225 --> 00:38:00.100
The one big difference
here is we've actually

00:38:00.100 --> 00:38:02.950
leveraged another VPC
that we're terming here

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:05.350
in this presentation
a connectivity VPC.

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:08.320
Now, this is not a separate
type of VPC that you check a box

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:10.292
and say, I want this
to be connectivity.

00:38:10.292 --> 00:38:12.250
This just happens to be
a normal VPC that we're

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:14.002
using in a very specific way.

00:38:14.002 --> 00:38:15.460
So what we've
actually done here is

00:38:15.460 --> 00:38:18.490
we're actually moving all of
the hybrid connectivity out

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:21.280
of the individual
environment-based projects,

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:25.090
and we're putting that
into this connectivity VPC.

00:38:25.090 --> 00:38:27.340
And then we peer
the connectivity VPC

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:31.060
with all of those
environment-based projects.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:32.590
So this actually
relies on a feature

00:38:32.590 --> 00:38:33.965
that we've just
recently released

00:38:33.965 --> 00:38:38.050
called VPC peering custom routes
that allows us to propagate

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:39.700
routes that are
learned dynamically

00:38:39.700 --> 00:38:42.370
from your on-premises
environment across VPC

00:38:42.370 --> 00:38:44.020
peering relationships.

00:38:44.020 --> 00:38:46.270
So the routes that you
advertise into the Google Cloud

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:48.130
environment that
specify what networks

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:50.130
in your on-prem
environment are going

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:51.842
to be reachable
from the cloud, we

00:38:51.842 --> 00:38:53.800
can now propagate those
routes all the way down

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:56.380
to these environment-based VPCs.

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:58.750
So a pretty powerful
capability, nice way

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:01.360
to not only leverage the
same physical connectivity,

00:39:01.360 --> 00:39:04.330
but to really remove a lot of
the kind of hybrid connectivity

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:08.675
constructs out of
those environment VPCs.

00:39:08.675 --> 00:39:10.300
Another common thing
I see as customers

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:12.640
grow, a lot of customers,
especially larger enterprises,

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:15.100
they may have multiple business
units or business entities

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:16.800
inside the organization.

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.600
And those entities pretty much
want to operate autonomously

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:22.600
within the cloud, except
when it comes to paying

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:23.770
for hybrid connectivity.

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:25.487
They almost always
want to share that.

00:39:25.487 --> 00:39:27.820
So I want to use the same
kind of high speed connections

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:29.800
I have with the Google
Cloud environment

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:32.410
even though I may be an entirely
different business entity.

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:34.540
So where we have
this segregation here

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:37.450
by environment type, like
dev, test, and production,

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:40.540
you could also think about that
to be separate logical business

00:39:40.540 --> 00:39:44.770
entities or small peer groups of
VPCs for a particular business

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:45.940
entity.

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:47.620
Name them whatever you want.

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:50.170
Inevitably in that case,
there's almost always

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:53.560
services that you want to share
across those business groups,

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:57.280
whether it's active directory,
or source code repositories,

00:39:57.280 --> 00:40:00.460
or the CI/CD pipeline,
those resources

00:40:00.460 --> 00:40:03.520
are also a good fit for
this connectivity VPC

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:06.550
because it has peer to access
to all of those downstream VPCs

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:07.360
as well.

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:08.860
So this acts--
again, this is really

00:40:08.860 --> 00:40:10.780
just about how you
use the networking

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:13.240
constructs in the cloud versus
just going and saying this

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:16.310
is going to be like
a connectivity VPC.

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:18.160
The scope and use
of the VPC really

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:20.347
defines its purpose inside
of the cloud environment.

00:40:20.347 --> 00:40:22.180
MATT NOWINA: So it's
not like you're limited

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:24.350
to a single connectivity VPC.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:26.322
This is actually a
scale-out pattern.

00:40:26.322 --> 00:40:28.030
ZACH SEILS: Yeah, you
could scale it out.

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:30.060
I'm working with
one customer now

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:33.060
where they have 13 independent
business entities inside

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:34.920
of their organization.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:40.150
Each one has their own kind of
connectivity VPC, if you will,

00:40:40.150 --> 00:40:42.570
and then they have a set of
small group of downstream VPCs

00:40:42.570 --> 00:40:45.820
that are environmentalized,
so dev, stage, production,

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:46.400
et cetera.

00:40:49.100 --> 00:40:51.520
So I think we kind of--
we have one more, right?

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:52.060
One more.

00:40:52.060 --> 00:40:54.520
So I think maybe we'll do
a little curve ball here,

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.530
just thinking about
what customers commonly

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:57.550
kind of come to us with.

00:40:57.550 --> 00:41:00.730
I'm going to give this one
to you just because I can.

00:41:00.730 --> 00:41:03.640
So what if a customer needs
to bring a third-party network

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:06.470
capability or device into the
cloud as a virtual machine?

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:08.520
MATT NOWINA: Why would
you ever need that?

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.600
ZACH SEILS: Let's
just say it's needed.

00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:12.340
How would that
influence and change

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:14.170
the way you're doing VPC
design and some of the things

00:41:14.170 --> 00:41:15.080
that we've talked about?

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:16.538
MATT NOWINA: So I
mean, wouldn't it

00:41:16.538 --> 00:41:19.720
be nice if there were cloud
native solutions for everything

00:41:19.720 --> 00:41:20.230
we wanted?

00:41:20.230 --> 00:41:23.810
But I mean, the reality is
that for any number of reasons

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:28.420
there are going to be times when
you need to bring appliances

00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:29.050
into the cloud.

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:31.300
You need to start to think
about what those deployment

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:32.360
models look like.

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:34.590
Now, the icons are a
little small at this point,

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:36.840
because we're starting to
group these things together.

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:39.040
But what we wanted to
sort of call out here

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:42.220
is the idea that
typically, these devices

00:41:42.220 --> 00:41:44.170
require multi-NICs.

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:48.153
So you can think of an NGFW that
you wanted to do layer seven

00:41:48.153 --> 00:41:49.570
inspection on, or
something that's

00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:53.130
going to act as a router
between your various VPCs.

00:41:53.130 --> 00:41:55.870
But there are specific
rules within GCP

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:59.540
that actually require us
to modify the VPC design.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:02.950
So here, what you're seeing
is that multi-NIC requires

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:07.840
different VPCs for each
of the interface cards,

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:11.047
and all of those VPCs have
to be in the same project.

00:42:11.047 --> 00:42:12.880
ZACH SEILS: So sorry,
Matt, just one second.

00:42:12.880 --> 00:42:15.040
So when you say
multi-NIC devices,

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:18.210
you're talking about a virtual
appliance for a security vendor

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:18.820
or something?

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:20.020
So it's multiple interfaces.

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:20.520
MATT NOWINA: Exactly.

00:42:20.520 --> 00:42:21.145
ZACH SEILS: OK.

00:42:21.145 --> 00:42:27.690
MATT NOWINA: Yeah So the best
practices, the considerations

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:30.090
you need to bring into
play when you're thinking

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:33.480
about these devices, though,
is how are you routing traffic

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:37.050
to those devices, are you
modifying the default route,

00:42:37.050 --> 00:42:40.290
what are the implications to
accessing managed services when

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:42.120
you're no longer using
the default internet

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:45.060
gateway, instead using
a third-party device,

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:48.000
as well as thinking
about high availability.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:51.060
So how are you ensuring
that this device

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:53.890
is up and forwarding packets?

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:55.650
How do you health check it?

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:58.110
Each one of these are
important considerations,

00:42:58.110 --> 00:43:01.290
but there is a deployment
pattern for using these devices

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:03.002
within GCP.

00:43:03.002 --> 00:43:03.960
ZACH SEILS: Good stuff.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:06.540
So I think we've covered a
number of different scenarios.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:08.040
I think this is
pretty commonly what

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:10.230
we see from customers in
terms of how they start

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:14.190
their journey really specific
to networking inside the cloud.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.350
Again, the premise here
is just start simple.

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:19.290
It's easy to kind
of expand and evolve

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:22.380
over time as your requirements
here need change in the cloud

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:23.070
environment.

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:27.120
There's no reason to kind of
overly engineer from a design

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:28.110
perspective.

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:30.750
Also, think less about
the traditional networking

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:34.890
constructs like placement in
the network and IP addresses

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:38.880
and subnet membership as really
kind of primary identifier

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:40.855
of a particular workload.

00:43:40.855 --> 00:43:42.480
As Matt mentioned,
we have capabilities

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:44.490
within the firewall to
do micro-segmentation

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:47.340
that can actually follow a
particular virtual machine

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:49.800
instance or humanities
pod regardless

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:52.050
of where it happens to be
instantiated in the network.

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:54.810
So this makes actually
creating the policies

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:57.540
and maintaining those policies
inside of our environment

00:43:57.540 --> 00:43:59.550
much, much more simple
by leveraging these kind

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:01.580
of abstractions for identity.

00:44:01.580 --> 00:44:03.700
And so, I think that--

00:44:03.700 --> 00:44:04.950
we have something else, right?

00:44:04.950 --> 00:44:07.770
You have something else
you want to talk about.

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:10.590
MATT NOWINA: So I almost
wish I had a black turtleneck

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:12.900
on for this part.

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:14.970
As much as Zach and
I would like to think

00:44:14.970 --> 00:44:20.130
that a 40-minute presentation on
some example of VPC deployment

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:23.700
scenarios would be enough
for you to sort of rethink

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:28.030
your designs, we actually
have gone one step further.

00:44:28.030 --> 00:44:31.680
So we believe that as a
cloud service provider,

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:34.380
the obligation is on
us to provide best

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:35.600
practices for these things.

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:37.142
You would have seen,
as we were going

00:44:37.142 --> 00:44:39.150
through these
deployments, that we

00:44:39.150 --> 00:44:41.280
had different sets
of best practices

00:44:41.280 --> 00:44:42.420
that go down the side.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:46.110
So I am very pleased to
announce during this session

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:50.370
that we have just made live our
VPC best practices and design

00:44:50.370 --> 00:44:51.910
guide.

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:54.360
So the links that
you see up here

00:44:54.360 --> 00:44:57.030
